{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>And one who carries its carcass.</b> Carrying-impurity is more severe than contact-impurity, for the one who carries defiles garments, but the one who touches — his garments are not impure, for 'he shall wash his garments' is not said of him." "<b>And one who eats of its carcass.</b> Might one say that eating defiles him? When it says concerning the carcass of a clean bird (Leviticus 22) ״נְבֵלָה וּטְרֵפָה לֹא יֹאכַל לְטָמְאָה בָהּ״ — 'בָהּ' — that one defiles garments through eating it, but the carcass of an animal does not defile garments through eating without carrying — for instance, if another person stuffed it into his mouth. If so, why does Scripture say 'the one who eats'? To set a measure for the one who carries and for the one who touches: the volume of an olive's worth (Sifra; Niddah 42)." "<b>And impure until evening.</b> Even though he immersed, he requires sunset."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:06:00.404306167Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}